                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Ok. This game is... I guess confusing is the best way to describe it. It's not inherently difficult, but there's just no rhyme or reason to it that I can find. (Granted, I've only finished the first two chapters... Of a currently unknown total to me).\n\n[h1]Gameplay[/h1]\nGameplay consists of running around with a torch that needs slapping every 20-30 seconds. You run around and eventually find a note that tells you to go somewhere, so you go there and pick something up, run around trying to work out where you use it. Then the cycle repeats. You'll find places you want to use thing long before you find what you need to use. That's pretty much all I've picked up so far in regards to gameplay. \n\n[h1]Controls[/h1]\nMove with WASD, toggle torch with Q, slap torch (because that makes batteries last forever) with R. There's a map on the first chapter but it doesn't tell you where you are, so it's mostly guess work. All fairly by the book. Shift is run. Remember that, it's the key you'll be holding on to for dear life because of...\n\n[h1]Enemies[/h1]\nIn Chapter 1, the story has only just started so you get the easy to avoid enemies such as:\n[list]\n[*]Mosquitos(?) That can kill you ridiculously quickly and blend into the rest of the floating debris so you don't know you're in the middle of them until it's too late. To avoid: Stick to the path.\n[*] The assistant, or what was, [i]your[/i] assistant. He's faster than you, can run farther than you and when he hits you, the screen flies into such jaunty angles, it's difficult to see where you're actually going. Seriously, you'll die. A lot because you need to run to [b]exact[/b] places for him to despawn and it's all just guesswork.\n[/list]\n\nChapter Two introduces two more enemies (though you only see one once during a scene). The other is a floating man...thing that you have to run away from for two minutes before being allowed to continue. It's annoying since, as well as the features of The Assistant, he can teleport right in front of you without warning.\n\n[h1]Story[/h1]\nSo... You're a corrupt politician who's on the run for something (it might have been explained, but I've forgotten if it was). Anyway, you’re in the woods and everything goes South, fast. Then you find out some woman died [strike]and was all pished off about it (who wouldn’t be!?)[/strike] and so yeah. You’re in the woods.\n\n[h1]Audio[/h1]\nSomething not inherently bad about the story, unless you can’t read, is that it’s all in Spanish. There’s no audible language feature. So you have to manually read everything. Figured it was worth noting since the Spanish – English translation could use some work. It’s not unreadable but it’s not good by general standards.\nThe Enemies seem to have a single SFX, so the repeated chases by The Assistant carry a monotonous “generic groaning noise” making the experience even more dull.\n\n[h1]Overview[/h1]\nI think I got this game as part of a bundle, when I checked the receipt, I think it ended up costing me about £1. I’ve, so far, put 6hours into it... which is really the first chapter twice (because I had no idea where I was going and gave up eventually first time) and Chapter 2 (replaying most of it because the Checkpoints are few and far between. Mix that with The Assistant and you get the idea).\nEither way, if I had the opportunity to, I’d refund it. It’s listed as £7 and I’d have been kicking myself if I paid that much for it. I don’t think I’ve enjoyed playing it as I’ve persevered.",
